<h2>A long-running eviction case</h2>
<p>The landlady sought eviction of a shop of roughly eleven by twelve feet for three pleaded needs: her husband&rsquo;s computer institute, her LIC agency work, and family residence. The tenant obtained leave to defend in 2013 and attacked the bona fides, calling the institute a sham; during the proceedings he also brought on record that the landlady had shifted to an upper floor and that the institute had closed. The Rent Controller nonetheless allowed eviction in April 2019. Then came the turn that defined the revision: in April 2021, during its pendency, the husband and the landlady died within days of each other. Their daughter was substituted in 2024, and the tenant pressed applications contending that the deaths had extinguished the need.</p>

<h2>The framework the Court applied</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The date that matters.</strong> Bona fide requirement is ordinarily assessed as on the date of the eviction petition (<em>Gaya Prasad v. Pradeep Srivastava</em>, (2001) 2 SCC 604; <em>Kamleshwar Prasad</em>, (1997) 4 SCC 413).</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The narrow door for later events.</strong> Subsequent developments count only where they completely eclipse the pleaded requirement — a high threshold drawn from the <em>Hasmat Rai</em> line of authority.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Death after the order.</strong> Under <em>Shakuntala Bai v. Narayan Das</em>, (2004) 5 SCC 772, a landlord&rsquo;s death after an eviction order does not by itself undo it; the legal representatives may defend its benefit.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The supervisory lens.</strong> Revision under the proviso to Section 25B(8) is not an appeal: <em>Abid-Ul-Islam v. Inder Sain Dua</em>, (2022) 6 SCC 30, confines the High Court to illegality, material irregularity and jurisdictional error.</div>
</div>

<h2>Applying it to the record</h2>
<p>Justice Harish Vaidyanathan Shankar took the tenant&rsquo;s subsequent-event applications on record but found they did not meet the eclipse threshold. Three needs had been pleaded, and the residential need was not personal to the deceased couple; the daughter, as sole heir, could claim its benefit, the Court reading &ldquo;own use&rdquo; in Section 14(1)(e) to embrace the legitimate needs of family members, including a parent&rsquo;s obligation to settle children. The Court distinguished the authorities the tenant invoked — cases where the requirement had demonstrably diminished or was purely personal to the deceased landlord. The tenant&rsquo;s factual counters, such as the allegation that another shop lay locked and available, failed for want of cogent material: no rent agreement or comparable evidence showed the alternative premises suitable. Within the supervisory limits of Section 25B(8), there was no illegality or material irregularity to correct, and the revision was dismissed.</p>

<h2>What the decision signals</h2>
<p>Eviction litigation under Section 14(1)(e) frequently outlives the people who begin it. This judgment consolidates the position that time and mortality do not automatically reset the clock: the tenant who litigates for a decade cannot treat the landlord&rsquo;s death as a windfall defence unless the need itself has genuinely vanished. For landlords&rsquo; families, the practical step is prompt substitution of legal representatives and a clear articulation of how the pleaded need survives in them. For tenants, a subsequent-events plea must be built on evidence that the requirement is gone — not merely that the original claimant is.</p>
<div class="note">Rent control eviction on bona fide requirement follows the summary procedure of Section 25B, with leave to defend granted only on triable grounds. The supervisory revision is the tenant&rsquo;s only challenge; no appeal lies. Each case turns on its own evidence of need and suitability of alternatives, and this article is informational only.</div>
